From 18f0ecd2197f31cfa5ed97d017c625a0c0cd8991 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Ewen <ryan.ewen@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 19:04:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] agentHost: admit a client tool call on the runtime's
 invocation

A client tool call had two independent triggers and no single record of
ownership. The stream mapper's ready action drove the session-input-needed
contribution to publish an execution request, while the in-process MCP handler
separately parked the SDK. Nothing tied "a client owns this call" to "the runtime
is parked waiting for it", so an ownerless or unclaimed call could hang, a
replayed call had no owner, and waiting for a claim was unbounded.

Add a `client_tool_invoked` provider signal carrying the root chat, the SDK
`tool_use_id`, the tool name, the input, and a subagent's `parentToolCallId`. The
Claude session fires it from `registerAndFire` where the MCP handler parks, so a
result buffered before the SDK asked for it short-circuits without firing.
Providers opt in through `IAgent.drivesClientToolExecution`; Copilot and Codex
keep executing on the streamed ready.

AgentSideEffects owns the admission record (invoked, admitted, settled,
abandoned) and stamps the admission into the tool call's protocol `_meta`, so it
survives a state snapshot and a client reconnect. The session-input-needed
contribution publishes a client execution request only for an admitted call, and
only for a provider that opts in. A call the runtime replays with no prior state
has its start and ready synthesized with the owner; a call no connected client
provides is failed once and forwarded once as `toolUnavailable`; an admitted call
no connected client claims within the existing client-disconnect window is failed
the same way, while a call whose owner is still connected is left to run.

The disconnect window moves from protocolServerHandler to
agentHostClientConnectionService so both uses share one number, and a rebind now
keeps buffered results instead of discarding an answer the SDK is about to ask
for again.
